+### Server load for clients (`GET /v1/load`)
+
+`GET /v1/load` (also `GET /load`; same API key rule as `/v1/status`) says how busy the server is, so a client can
+hold back work that is not urgent. `POST /v1/load` and `POST /load` still load the model (see Sharing the GPU); only
+`GET` reports.
+
+```json
+{"load_pct": 75, "level": "busy", "would_wait": false, "image_alone": false, "loaded": true,
+ "slots": {"total": 16, "busy": 12, "decoding": 10, "reading_prompt": 2, "free": 4, "waiting": 0},
+ "tok_s": {"now": 250.0, "peak": 660.0, "share_pct": 38}, "server_load": 12,
+ "advice": "defer non-urgent work", "time": 1791900000.0}
+```
+
+- `load_pct` is the larger of two shares: the token rate of the last 3 s against the 100 % mark, and the slots in use
+  against all slots. `level`: `idle` below 25 %, `low` below 50 %, `busy` below 80 %, `saturated` from there.
+- `slots.waiting` counts every request that waits for room once: for the control lines (one prompt read at a time), for
+  a slot, or before it is registered. A request that is already in counts as running, not waiting: a read that gave
+  way, one back from its slot to the solo path, and a `STRATA_COALESCE_PREFILL` follower whose prompt is read into its
+  slot. `would_wait`: a new request would wait now.
+- The 100 % mark (`tok_s.peak`) is the highest token rate **held for 10 s while at least half the slots decoded** (one
+  request's speed is not the server's capacity, and a one-second figure can be a burst). It is kept between runs in
+  the file `STRATA_LOAD_PEAK_FILE` names, or in `<config>.load-peak.json` when that file exists (create an empty one
+  to start; without either the mark is kept in memory only), with the engine settings it was measured with; a peak
+  measured with another engine or config is not taken. `STRATA_LOAD_REF_TOK_S=<tok/s>` sets the
+  mark by hand. Until one is known, `tok_s.peak` is `null` and only the slots count.
+- `server_load` is vLLM's `GET /load` figure: the requests in flight, queued ones included.
+
